---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Icon from '../../components/Icon.astro';
import Katex from '../../components/Katex.astro';
import '../../styles/deepDive.css';
import 'katex/dist/katex.min.css';

const title = "Causal Inference in Human Genetics: Mendelian Randomization (MR)";
const description = "A mathematical and epidemiological guide to Mendelian Randomization (MR) — evaluating the 3 core instrumental variable assumptions, the Inverse-Variance Weighted (IVW) estimator, MR-Egger regression, Weighted Median, and MR-PRESSO pleiotropy corrections.";
const readingTime = "19 min read";
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} type="article">
  <article class="deep-dive-article">
    <!-- Back to Master Guide -->
    <nav style="margin-bottom: var(--space-4);">
      <a href="/deep_dives/statistical-genetics/" style="display: inline-flex; align-items: center; gap: 0.35rem; font-size: var(--fs-small); color: var(--color-accent); font-weight: 600;">
        <Icon name="arrow-left" size={14} /> Back to Statistical Genetics Master Guide
      </a>
    </nav>

    <!-- Header -->
    <header class="deep-dive-header">
      <div class="deep-dive-meta-bar" style="margin-bottom: var(--space-2);">
        <span class="deep-dive-tag">Statistical Genetics · Part 9</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-level-badge"><Icon name="book-open" size={14} /> Advanced & Causal</span>
        <span class="deep-dive-time-badge"><Icon name="clock" size={14} /> {readingTime}</span>
      </div>
      <h1 class="deep-dive-title">{title}</h1>
      <p class="deep-dive-meta-bar">
        <span>By <strong>Kuan-Hao Chao</strong></span>
        <span>·</span>
        <span>Published August 2026</span>
      </p>
    </header>

    <!-- Lead / Introduction -->
    <p class="deep-dive-lead">
      In observational epidemiology, correlation rarely implies causation: lifestyle factors, socioeconomic status, and environmental variables create severe confounding, while prodromal disease states introduce reverse causation. <strong>Mendelian Randomization (MR)</strong> uses inherited genetic variants as <strong>Instrumental Variables (IVs)</strong> to test whether an exposure (e.g. LDL cholesterol, circulating biomarkers) causally drives a clinical outcome (e.g. coronary artery disease). This deep dive explores the mathematical machinery, core assumptions, and pleiotropy-robust estimators of modern MR.
    </p>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="deep-dive-toc">
      <div class="deep-dive-toc-title">
        <Icon name="list" size={14} /> Table of Contents
      </div>
      <ol class="deep-dive-toc-list">
        <li><a href="#rct">1. Nature's Randomized Controlled Trial</a></li>
        <li><a href="#assumptions">2. The Three Core Instrumental Variable (IV) Assumptions</a></li>
        <li><a href="#ivw">3. The Inverse-Variance Weighted (IVW) Estimator</a></li>
        <li><a href="#horizontal-pleiotropy">4. The Threat of Horizontal Pleiotropy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#mr-egger">5. MR-Egger Regression: Testing Directional Pleiotropy</a></li>
        <li><a href="#weighted-median">6. The Weighted Median & MR-PRESSO Estimators</a></li>
        <li><a href="#summary">7. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</a></li>
      </ol>
    </div>

    <!-- Chapter 1 -->
    <section id="rct">
      <h2>1. Nature's Randomized Controlled Trial</h2>
      <p>
        During meiosis, parental alleles are randomly segregated and independently assorted into gametes (Mendel's Laws). Because an individual's genotype is fixed at conception:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>No Reverse Causation:</strong> Developing a disease later in life cannot alter the germline DNA sequence inherited at fertilization.</li>
        <li><strong>Confounder Protection:</strong> Because gametic segregation is random, genetic alleles are generally independent of external environmental confounders (e.g. diet, exercise, socioeconomic status).</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Thus, Mendelian Randomization acts as an observational analogue to a lifelong <strong>Randomized Controlled Trial (RCT)</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 2 -->
    <section id="assumptions">
      <h2>2. The Three Core Instrumental Variable (IV) Assumptions</h2>
      <p>
        For a genetic variant <Katex tex="Z" /> to serve as a valid instrumental variable for exposure <Katex tex="X" /> on outcome <Katex tex="Y" />, three strict mathematical conditions must hold:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-callout callout-math">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="check-circle" size={15} /> The Triad of Instrumental Variable Invariants
        </div>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>1. The Relevance Assumption:</strong> The variant <Katex tex="Z" /> must be robustly associated with the exposure <Katex tex="X" />. To guard against weak-instrument bias, the first-stage regression <Katex tex="F" />-statistic must satisfy:
            <Katex tex="F = \frac{R^2 (N - 1 - K)}{(1 - R^2) K} > 10" display={true} />
          </li>
          <li><strong>2. The Independence / Exchangeability Assumption:</strong> The instrument <Katex tex="Z" /> must be independent of all unmeasured confounders <Katex tex="U" /> that bias the exposure-outcome relationship: <Katex tex="Z \perp U" />.
          </li>
          <li><strong>3. The Exclusion Restriction:</strong> The instrument <Katex tex="Z" /> must affect the outcome <Katex tex="Y" /> <strong>strictly through its effect on exposure <Katex tex="X" /></strong>, with zero direct pathways (no horizontal pleiotropy): <Katex tex="Y \perp Z \mid X, U" />.
          </li>
        </ol>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 3 -->
    <section id="ivw">
      <h2>3. The Inverse-Variance Weighted (IVW) Estimator</h2>
      <p>
        For a single genetic instrument <Katex tex="j" />, the Wald ratio estimator computes the ratio of the variant-outcome association to the variant-exposure association:
        <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{\text{Wald}, j} = \frac{\hat{\beta}_{Yj}}{\hat{\beta}_{Xj}}" />.
      </p>
      <p>
        When combining <Katex tex="K" /> independent genetic instruments, the <strong>Inverse-Variance Weighted (IVW)</strong> estimator calculates a meta-analytic weighted average:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">IVW Master Causal Estimator</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{\text{IVW}} = \frac{\sum_{j=1}^{K} w_j \hat{\beta}_{Xj} \hat{\beta}_{Yj}}{\sum_{j=1}^{K} w_j \hat{\beta}_{Xj}^2}, \qquad w_j = \frac{1}{\text{Var}(\hat{\beta}_{Yj}) \approx \text{SE}(\hat{\beta}_{Yj})^2}" display={true} />
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 4 -->
    <section id="horizontal-pleiotropy">
      <h2>4. The Threat of Horizontal Pleiotropy</h2>
      <p>
        In complex human traits, many genetic variants are <strong>pleiotropic</strong>: a variant might alter exposure <Katex tex="X" /> (e.g. HDL cholesterol) but also directly alter an alternative biological pathway that influences outcome <Katex tex="Y" /> (e.g. vascular inflammation).
      </p>
      <p>
        If horizontal pleiotropy is directional (nonzero on average), the standard IVW estimator becomes severely biased and will produce false-positive causal inferences.
      </p>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 5 -->
    <section id="mr-egger">
      <h2>5. MR-Egger Regression: Testing Directional Pleiotropy</h2>
      <p>
        <strong>Bowden et al. (2015)</strong> adapted Egger's meta-analysis regression to Mendelian Randomization by allowing for an unconstrained intercept <Katex tex="\beta_0" />:
      </p>

      <div class="deep-dive-equation-card">
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-header">MR-Egger Regression Model</div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-body">
          <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{Yj} = \beta_0 + \beta_{\text{Egger}} \hat{\beta}_{Xj} + \epsilon_j" display={true} />
        </div>
        <div class="deep-dive-equation-legend">
          <strong>Key Invariants:</strong><br/>
          &bull; <strong>The InSIDE Assumption:</strong> Instrument Strength Independent of Direct Effect (pleiotropic effects are uncorrelated with exposure effect sizes).<br/>
          &bull; <strong>Egger Intercept Test:</strong> If <Katex tex="\beta_0 \neq 0" /> (<Katex tex="P < 0.05" />), directional horizontal pleiotropy is present.<br/>
          &bull; <strong>Egger Slope (<Katex tex="\beta_{\text{Egger}}" />):</strong> Provides a consistent, pleiotropy-corrected causal effect estimate even when directional pleiotropy is present.
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 6 -->
    <section id="weighted-median">
      <h2>6. The Weighted Median & MR-PRESSO Estimators</h2>
      <div class="paper-table-wrap">
        <table class="paper-table">
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Method</th>
              <th>Algorithmic Mechanism</th>
              <th>Breakdown Point / Robustness Guarantee</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Weighted Median (Bowden 2016)</strong></td>
              <td>Sorts individual Wald ratio estimates <Katex tex="\hat{\beta}_{Yj}/\hat{\beta}_{Xj}" /> and takes the median weighted by <Katex tex="w_j" />.</td>
              <td>Consistent as long as <strong><Katex tex="\ge 50\%" /> of the total genetic weight comes from valid instruments</strong>.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>MR-PRESSO (Verbanck 2018)</strong></td>
              <td>Global residual sum-of-squares test identifies specific outlier instruments causing excessive heterogeneity; removes outliers and recalibrates IVW.</td>
              <td>Robust to discrete outlier pleiotropy; reports a formal outlier-corrected causal p-value.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td><strong>Multivariable MR (MVMR)</strong></td>
              <td>Jointly models multiple correlated exposures (e.g. LDL-C, HDL-C, and Triglycerides) in a single multivariable regression.</td>
              <td>Isolates the direct causal effect of each exposure conditioned on all others.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
    </section>

    <!-- Chapter 7 -->
    <section id="summary">
      <h2>7. Summary & Next in the Statistical Genetics Series</h2>
      <p>
        Mendelian Randomization establishes causal directions in epidemiological associations. In the final chapter of our series, we examine the grand synthesis: how <strong>deep sequence models and genomic language models</strong> are revolutionizing modern statistical genetics:
      </p>
      <div class="deep-dive-callout">
        <div class="deep-dive-callout-title">
          <Icon name="arrow-right" size={15} /> Continue to Part 10
        </div>
        <p>
          <a href="/deep_dives/statgen-deep-learning-synthesis/" style="font-weight: 700; color: var(--color-accent);">
            Part 10: The Modern Synthesis: Statistical Genetics Meets Deep Learning & Foundation Models &rarr;
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
